WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.760
CRAIG BOX: Anthos is Google
Cloud's new open platform

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:06.420
that lets you run an
app anywhere, simply,

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:08.280
flexibly, and securely.

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:11.480
With Anthos, we bring
the cloud to you.

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:13.230
You will have heard a
lot about Kubernetes

00:00:13.230 --> 00:00:14.550
in the last few years.

00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:16.720
I mean, it's five
years old this year,

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:19.440
so why aren't you running
it at your company yet?

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:21.840
Maybe you feel that Kubernetes
is meant more for startups

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:23.760
or digital companies,
because not everyone has

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:26.640
the luxury of moving
immediately to the cloud.

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:27.600
And let's be clear.

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:29.670
Your company does want
to move to the cloud.

00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:31.900
It's on the long term plan.

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:34.410
The reality is your team
has faced many roadblocks.

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:36.840
You have an on-prem environment
full of legacy systems,

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:39.660
and you don't feel ready to
make the move to the cloud.

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:41.400
Some systems can't
move to the cloud

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:45.293
for one reason or another due to
regulatory or compliance needs.

00:00:45.293 --> 00:00:46.710
From what you've
learned, it seems

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:49.298
like running Kubernetes
on-prem is challenging.

00:00:49.298 --> 00:00:51.840
There are a multitude of options
to run it, including hundred

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:54.180
or so do it yourself methods.

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:56.730
And the project maintains
a fast release cycle.

00:00:56.730 --> 00:00:59.052
Those updates provide
helpful security features,

00:00:59.052 --> 00:01:00.510
but that also means
needing someone

00:01:00.510 --> 00:01:03.160
to manage and frequently
roll out updates.

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:05.459
That's like giving someone
a puppy for their birthday.

00:01:05.459 --> 00:01:08.580
They'll love it, but they'll
have to do a ton of work.

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:11.430
You want to care about keeping
services up and operational,

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:14.520
and making sure there are
no security incidents.

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:17.610
While your developers think
about binaries and containers,

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:21.192
as an operator, you are
concerned about SLAs and users.

00:01:21.192 --> 00:01:23.400
You're worried if you make
it too easy to run things,

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:25.260
your developers could
choose to run anything

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:27.402
risking destandardization.

00:01:27.402 --> 00:01:28.860
As an administrator,
you need to be

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:31.770
able to dictate policies and
decide which apps can talk

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:33.480
to which network endpoints.

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:35.670
For example, requiring
applications to be

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:37.810
signed to run.

00:01:37.810 --> 00:01:39.490
And then on top
of that, you want

00:01:39.490 --> 00:01:42.380
observability of your
applications and services.

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:44.950
This is where GKE
On-Prem comes into play.

00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:46.660
It lets you take
advantage of Kubernetes

00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:49.738
and cloud technology
in your data center.

00:01:49.738 --> 00:01:51.280
The creators of
Kubernetes have built

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:54.550
an enterprise-grade environment
with the lifecycle and upgrades

00:01:54.550 --> 00:01:56.590
managed by Google engineers.

00:01:56.590 --> 00:02:00.220
GKE On-Prem installs into your
existing vSphere environment.

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:01.810
You create a
cluster, and then you

00:02:01.810 --> 00:02:05.570
can run the entire ecosystem
of Kubernetes tools with it.

00:02:05.570 --> 00:02:07.320
You can manage the
applications you deploy

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:10.660
to a GKE On-Prem environment
using the Google Cloud Console

00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:14.700
that you use for cloud workloads
giving you unified management.

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:16.450
This also means when
you are ready to move

00:02:16.450 --> 00:02:18.520
to a hybrid architecture,
you can very easily

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:20.830
move your applications to
the same platform running

00:02:20.830 --> 00:02:24.650
in the cloud using
GKE on Google Cloud.

00:02:24.650 --> 00:02:26.390
When you choose
Google, we give you

00:02:26.390 --> 00:02:28.130
a solution that lets
you adopt Kubernetes

00:02:28.130 --> 00:02:30.170
and its many benefits
without necessarily

00:02:30.170 --> 00:02:32.870
having to move immediately
to the public cloud.

00:02:32.870 --> 00:02:34.880
You will enjoy faster
time to market,

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:38.600
lower administrative overhead,
and increased innovation.

00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:40.130
Plus, you have
consistent management

00:02:40.130 --> 00:02:43.790
of applications deployed
in GKE on Google Cloud.

00:02:43.790 --> 00:02:45.380
You get best of
breed tools built

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:48.470
for how Google runs services
based on the Istio service

00:02:48.470 --> 00:02:50.760
mesh and Stackdriver.

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:52.530
By thinking services
first, you can

00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:54.930
automate policy and
security at scale

00:02:54.930 --> 00:02:58.170
at a higher layer in the stack
enabling greater awareness,

00:02:58.170 --> 00:03:01.330
consistency, and control
at the application level,

00:03:01.330 --> 00:03:03.342
not at the container level.

00:03:03.342 --> 00:03:05.550
And finally, you get access
to all the modern tooling

00:03:05.550 --> 00:03:08.670
for DevOps and SRE on top
of the Kubernetes platform,

00:03:08.670 --> 00:03:11.460
and easy deployment of popular
applications from the GCP

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:13.400
marketplace.

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:15.800
With GKE and GKE
On-Prem, you can

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:18.290
overcome the trade-offs once
faced with hybrid approaches

00:03:18.290 --> 00:03:19.580
to the cloud.

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:22.670
You get Kubernetes managed
by its creators at Google,

00:03:22.670 --> 00:03:25.610
and you can dedicate more time
to serving your own customers.

00:03:25.610 --> 00:03:27.950
To learn more about
Kubernetes and GKE, please

00:03:27.950 --> 00:03:30.100
subscribe to our channel.

